    "etymology": {
     "text": "From Proto-Semitic *ʔakal-; cognate with Hebrew אָכַל (akhal) and Aramaic ʾăḵal.",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "The imperative is just كُلْ (kul, ‘eat!’): like خُذْ (‘take!’) and مُرْ (‘order!’) it drops its first radical, the hamza."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "I eat = آكُلُ (ʾākulu): hamza + long ā is written with madda, آ.",
     "The vowel of the 2nd radical changes: a in the past (أَكَلَ), u in the present (يَأْكُلُ)."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "In formal Arabic, especially for meals, تَنَاوَلَ is preferred: تَنَاوَلْنَا الْغَدَاءَ (we had lunch)."
    ]
